73:where $p_{t}(\cdot \mid x)=\int p_{t \mid 0}\left(\cdot \mid \theta_{0}\right) p\left(\theta_{0} \mid x\right) \mathrm{d} \theta_{0}$ denotes the time marginal density of $\theta_{t}$, conditioned on $x$. By definition, the marginals of $\left(\bar{\theta}_{t}\right)_{t \in[0, T]} \mid x$ are equal to those of $\left(\theta_{T-t}\right)_{t \in[0, T]} \mid x$. Thus, in particular, $\bar{\theta}_{T} \sim p_{0}(\cdot \mid x):=$ $p(\cdot \mid x)$. Hence, if we could sample $\bar{\theta}_{0} \sim p_{T}(\cdot \mid x)$, and simulate $\left(\bar{\theta}_{t}\right)_{t \in[0, T]}$ according to (3), then its final distribution would be the desired posterior distribution. This process is visualised in Figure 1.
